From 78336b9ab0c3b36f18d9c5b94e0b814fe89df988 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 08:30:53 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] www/httpd: Warden saves posted multipart into a url-encoded
 parser

Field report, 2026-08-27, on v2.8.3: changing a Warden blocklist or pressing
Update now answered "invalid request token", and stayed broken across a logout
and back in. Reported from Firefox, but it was never a browser or a session
problem - the request simply never parsed, on every browser.

THE DEFECT. When ban/allow/cfeeds moved out of nvram into /jffs (they had hit
the silent 1 KB cap), Reaper_Warden.asp stopped putting them on the nvram apply
form and began POSTing them to the new save_lists endpoint. It built the body
with `new FormData()` and called `lx.send(lf)`, which sets multipart/form-data.
The endpoint's input handler is do_html_post_and_get, which is url-encoded-only:
it reads the body with a SINGLE fgets(), so it got the multipart boundary line
and nothing else. That line carries no '=', so init_cgi() registered ZERO
variables, http_id was never there, and the CSRF gate refused the request.

Scope was wider than the lists. rwSave() only calls f.submit() inside the XHR
success callback, so the abort killed the ENTIRE Warden save - master toggle,
countries, feed selection, logging - for both buttons. The live-stats poll kept
working throughout, because it sends the same token as a GET query string, which
is why the page looked healthy while nothing could be saved.

THE FIX is the transport, and it is the pattern already proven on applyapp.cgi
by Reaper_Advisor.asp: URLSearchParams, an explicit
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded, and send(lf.toString()). The
three values are single-line by construction (rwCleanCidrs joins on spaces,
rwCfeedCollect on '<'), so no literal newline can truncate that fgets either;
encoded worst case is ~34 KB against a 64 KB post_buf.

AND THE REASON THE REPORT COST A ROUND TRIP. The gate answered "invalid request
token" for three unrelated failures, so the one message could not distinguish a
body that never parsed from a genuine token mismatch - the user read it as a
dead session and logged out and back in twice before the report reached us. The
gate now classifies: init_cgi() counts the pairs it actually parsed (cgi_nvars()),
and reaper_gate()/reaper_gate_hid() return NODATA / TOKEN / XSITE. "request
carried no parameters" + ek _1B and "cross-site request refused" + ek _1C are new;
"invalid request token" + ek _1 keeps exactly its old meaning, so existing help
text is unaffected. All 15 Reaper gates converted. The security decision is
unchanged - anything but RGATE_OK is still refused.

Two gates needed their own handling: the .rbk restore upload takes its token
from a multipart field its own POST half captures, so it passes rbk_up.hid (an
array, never NULL) and can only return TOKEN/XSITE - cgi_nvars() is legitimately
0 there; the disk-unmount endpoint reads a JSON body and emits no error text, so
it refuses silently on any non-OK.

Verified into the staged filesystem, not just off a green build: fs/www/
Reaper_Warden.asp carries URLSearchParams with no FormData left, and
fs/usr/sbin/httpd carries all three classifier strings.

Swept the rest of www: the only other FormData users are Reaper_Advisor.asp
(wrapped in URLSearchParams) and Reaper_Firmware.asp (upgrade.cgi, which has a
real multipart handler), and no page sends a JSON body to a url-encoded CGI.

The sibling branches carry the same defect and still need the port.

EXTENDNO -> Reaper_v2.8.4. v2.8.3 is in the field; this rung takes its own
number so the two are never confused in a log.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
